{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "67",
    "case_summary": "A 32-year-old right-hand-dominant male manual laborer visited the Orthopaedic Outpatient Clinic with a two-year history of numbness and tingling in the palmar surface of his left hand and fingers. Over the past six months, he also experienced burning pain in the same area, which disrupted his sleep, and a progressive inability to perform his job. There was no history of trauma or other medical issues. Symptoms were linked to carpal tunnel syndrome, confirmed by median nerve conduction study (NCS). A classical open palmar approach was used to release the transverse carpal ligament. The surgeon's report noted severe compression of the median nerve but no other abnormalities in the dissected area. Postoperatively, the patient had only partial and temporary symptom relief without improvement in hand function. Three months later, he was referred to our department for further evaluation. Physical examination revealed tenderness and swelling on the palmar aspect of the left hand and the area of the previous incision, along with slight atrophy of the thenar muscles. Sensory loss was noted in the median nerve distribution compared to the opposite hand, and Tinel's sign was positive over the median nerve at the wrist. The patient exhibited decreased grip strength, and passive flexion and extension of the fingers were painful.\n\nMRI showed a volarly placed soft tissue mass with well-defined margins and an ovoid shape which was in direct contact with the flexor tendons. The histological examination of the surgical specimen revealed spindle-shaped cells arranged in fascicles with cigar-shaped nuclei and eosinophilic cytoplasm. The smooth muscle nature of the tumor was confirmed immunohistologically by a strong reactivity against smooth muscle actin (SMA) and a negative reaction against S-100 and epithelial membrane antigen (EMA). No significant cellular pleomorphism or mitotic activity was noted.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
